1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,993
Materializations are different ways how your models can be stored and managed in the data warehouse

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,996
There are four built in materializations View Table Incremental and Ephemeral

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:20,994
View is the default and this is the materialization you already saw in our previous lectures

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:31,000
When you pick the materialization view then your model will be represented as a view It's so simple

5
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:34,996
There are other materializations like the Table

6
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:42,994
If you pick the Table materialization your model will be materialized as an actual table

7
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:49,993
And every time you re execute your dbtflow this table will be recreated

8
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:00,212
Then there is an incremental materialization which is still a table but a materialization type created for incremental loads

9
00:01:00,212 --> 00:01:02,989
like event data or any fact data

10
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,992
In our case in our Airbnb use case this will be reviews

11
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:12,990
So as reviews flow in we will increment our tables with the new reviews

12
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:20,000
And then there is the Ephemeral materialization which is very much a virtual materialization

13
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,000
It is a no materialization materialization

14
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:29,000
It means that if you tell a model to be an Ephemeral materialization

15
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:35,995
it will be converted into a CTE in any downstream model

16
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,994
but it won't be in the data warehouse per se

17
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:43,994
So when do you want to use these materializations

18
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:49,992
I believe if you have experience with data warehousing it's very much common sense

19
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,000
But let me just sum it up real quick

20
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:59,994
So as you know a view materialization a view is a very lightweight thing in a data warehouse

21
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,000
It's more like a select which acts as if it was a table kind of right

22
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,993
So you want to use it if you want to have a lightweight representation

23
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:15,990
when you don't want to recreate a table at every execution

24
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:21,190
But you don't want to use it if you read from the same view

25
00:02:21,190 --> 00:02:24,996
many times repeatedly in the same pipeline

26
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,996
Because remember the view is just a select

27
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,400
Every time you access a view

28
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:35,000
the underlying select needs to be executed

29
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,076
If you want to have an efficient way to work

30
00:02:40,076 --> 00:02:45,998
with a materialization that can be accessed repeatedly

31
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,997
you probably want to go with the table materialization

32
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,994
Now what are the cons of the table materialization

33
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:57,991
It's that it needs to be recreated every time you execute your pipeline

34
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,306
So it takes some extra time Because when it's there

35
00:03:02,306 --> 00:03:05,380
your transform data is there

36
00:03:05,380 --> 00:03:09,989
so it will be quite fast to read from that data

37
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:15,085
But in many cases you have data which is incrementing

38
00:03:15,085 --> 00:03:17,992
so event level data right

39
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,876
For example orders in a store solution

40
00:03:21,876 --> 00:03:23,989
or in our case reviews

41
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:27,000
In this case you don't want to have your table

42
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,000
to be recreated every time

43
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:30,800
You only want to ingest the new data

44
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:33,000
and append it to the end of the table

45
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:35,996
So incremental materializations will help you there

46
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:42,987
And as you will see dbt manages much of the heavy lifting for you here

47
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:48,662
And then it can happen that you simply define an intermediate step

48
00:03:48,662 --> 00:03:52,250
between models and you just don't want to publish

49
00:03:52,250 --> 00:03:55,000
and advertise it in the data warehouse

50
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:59,997
And in this case ephemeral materializations can work very well

51
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,998
So we will see an example to all of those in the upcoming lessons So let's get started
